      });
    }

    /* ---- collapse: a floor left behind gives way, as floors do ----
       The floor is redrawn on a WebGL canvas exactly as its picture shows it,
       together with the blocks the picture shows (/art/cubes-N.bin, gated with
       it). It breaks into irregular chunks a few blocks across and deep. Each
       one cracks and settles, then drops, tipping over its outer edge,
       darkening as it falls into the dark and fading out to nothing; crumbs
       trickle from it and dust blooms where it broke. The underside and the rim
       go first, the middle of the surface last. Until its moment a chunk is the
       picture's own pixels, and once it moves it carries them with it, so the
       floor never changes its look when it starts to break, and nothing is
       left to vanish at the end. All of it follows the scroll: scrolling back
       up builds the floor again. Without WebGL, or before the blocks have
       arrived, the picture sinks and fades as a whole; with reduced motion
       nothing falls. */
    const LEAD = 0.07, DUR = 0.36;     // the crack before a chunk drops, and how long its fall lasts (in q)
